Structured output + speculative decoding: tokens accepted past the grammar's
termination point must be ignored, not fatal (issue #31).

A speculative verify window can legally accept tokens past the point where the
xgrammar matcher terminates: the matcher completes at the grammar's own end (a
tool call's closing tag, a JSON schema's final brace, or the stop token), and
the same step's remaining accepted tokens -- a newline after </tool_call>, the
stop token itself, anything after it under ignore_eos -- arrive at
accept_tokens() anyway. 0.27.1 treats both arrivals as failure:

  - a post-termination token inside one call fails matcher.accept_token()
    ("Failed to advance FSM ... Please file an issue", plus xgrammar's
    grammar_matcher.cc:612 warning), and returns False;
  - any call after a terminating call hits the _is_terminated early-return,
    which also returns False.

The scheduler turns that False into "Unexpected: grammar rejected tokens ...
Terminating request" -> FINISHED_ERROR, and the client gets an HTTP error for a
request whose output was completely valid. With DFLASH_TOKENS=15 the verify
window nearly always covers the tokens around the stop, so tool-calling under
SPEC=dflash2 hits this reliably (issue #31); reproduced here on the shipped
config with a json_schema + ignore_eos request:

  ERROR [backend_xgrammar.py:162] Failed to advance FSM ... for tokens 198.
  ERROR [scheduler.py:1835] Unexpected: grammar rejected tokens
        [16, 22, 198, 92, 248046, 198] ... Terminating request.

(16, 22 close the JSON, 92 is the brace that completes the grammar, 248046 is
the stop token that terminates the matcher, and the trailing 198 -- one newline
-- kills the request.)

Upstream vLLM has since changed accept_tokens to exactly the semantics below
("Tokens after termination are ignored"): break out of the loop the moment the
matcher terminates and report success, and report success (not failure) when
called on an already-terminated grammar. This is a straight backport. The rest
of the machinery is already consistent with it: _fill_bitmasks() masks nothing
for a terminated grammar, so a request that outlives its grammar decodes
unconstrained to its natural stop; the guidance backend already returns True
when its matcher is stopped; and validate_tokens() trims drafts against the
matcher without touching this path.

Genuine mid-grammar rejections (a token the FSM cannot accept before
termination) still log and still return False, so real integration bugs stay
loud.

Applies to the vLLM tree:
  patch -p1 -d venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/vllm < patches/0014-xgrammar-spec-terminated.patch

